ext","value":"The purpose of the next activity is to give you guided practice in finding the probability distribution of a discrete random variable.","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"CrDOnruBDY"}],"key":"yCnUsOZq0Z"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Building a Probability Distribution","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"anUoiL8vAq"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-building-a-probability-distribution","label":"Check Your Understanding: Building a Probability Distribution","html_id":"check-your-understanding-building-a-probability-distribution","implicit":true,"key":"Cowc2O1imo"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A young couple decides to try to have children until they have a boy. For financial reasons, the couple decides that they are going to stop trying when they have three children, whether they have a boy or not. (We are assuming that having a boy or a girl is equally likely, and that the child’s gender in each birth is independent of the gender in the other births.)","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"rKKfeZ5ofy"}],"key":"PkcacKUjJx"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let the random variable X be the number of children the couple has.","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"hxf8voR3Zy"}],"key":"jtUDLtZf8I"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Our goal is to find the probability distribution of X. In other words, we would like to create a table that lists all the possible values of X and the corresponding probabilities. We’ll follow the same steps we followed in the two examples we solved.","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"pfI7TwUd7q"}],"key":"tv2FualYQL"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-68378715a95f021281d81f443f976cac.mjs","model":{"question":"What are the possible outcomes of this \"experiment,\" and their probabilities?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"B (1/2), GB (1/4), GGB (1/8), GGG (1/8)","feedback":"Correct! P(B) = 1/2; P(GB) = (1/2)(1/2) = 1/4; P(GGB) = 1/8; P(GGG) = 1/8."},{"correct":false,"text":"B, BG, BB, GG — each with probability 1/4","feedback":"The couple stops after a boy, so sequences like BG or BB can't happen."},{"correct":false,"text":"B, GB, GGB, GGG — each with probability 1/4","feedback":"The four outcomes are not equally likely—stopping early makes B much more likely than GGB."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The couple stops at the first boy, or after three children. The possible sequences are B, GB, GGB, GGG.","explanation":""},"key":"udkVGu8aNT"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-68378715a95f021281d81f443f976cac.mjs","model":{"question":"What is P(X = 1), the probability that the couple has exactly one child?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"1/2","feedback":"Correct! X = 1 only for the outcome B, so P(X = 1) = 1/2."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/4","feedback":"1/4 is the probability of GB, which gives two children."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/8","feedback":"With four possible outcomes but unequal probabilities, don't use 1/4 per outcome."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Which outcome gives exactly one child?","explanation":""},"key":"DfNPjt16dy"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-68378715a95f021281d81f443f976cac.mjs","model":{"question":"What is P(X = 3), the probability that the couple has three children?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"1/4","feedback":"Correct! P(X = 3) = P(GGB) + P(GGG) = 1/8 + 1/8 = 1/4."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/8","feedback":"1/8 counts only one of the two outcomes with three children."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/2","feedback":"Check: the probability of two girls first is 1/4, and then the third child's gender doesn't change the count of children."}],"multi":false,"hint":"X = 3 happens for GGB or GGG.","explanation":""},"key":"FhWCU8Lgwm"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":82,"column":1},"end":{"line":82,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"So the probability distribution of X is:","position":{"start":{"line":82,"column":1},"end":{"line":82,"column":1}},"key":"yY48KRabqe"}],"key":"dHgdnnZuxp"},{"type":"table","position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":86,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"x","position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"key":"NQngMRE5DZ"}],"key":"w5g3gyXxNv"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"1","position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"key":"y6DE7sV7qC"}],"key":"vkXxtFox0g"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"2","position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"key":"dHob3ALrXc"}],"key":"IlKhufwpNE"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3","position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"key":"pbfezOeBO8"}],"key":"ZDk9FZ9QZR"}],"key":"ARPNnV9NAW"},{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":1},"end":{"line":86